1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:05,518
Part 1: You will hear a woman
phoning a railway company's

2
00:00:05,542 --> 00:00:09,012
lost property office about
a bag she left on a train.

3
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:13,580
First, you have some time
to look at questions 1 to 5.

4
00:00:43,899 --> 00:00:49,000
Part 2: Now listen carefully
and answer questions 1 to 5.

5
00:00:49,001 --> 00:00:52,799
Good afternoon. Westmere
Railway's lost property office.

6
00:00:52,823 --> 00:00:55,012
Gareth speaking. How can I help?

7
00:00:55,299 --> 00:00:58,278
Oh, hello. Um, I think I
left a bag on one of your

8
00:00:58,302 --> 00:01:01,412
trains and I'm really hoping
someone's handed it in.

9
00:01:01,580 --> 00:01:05,156
Right. Well, you've come to the
right place. I'll take a few details first

10
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:08,672
and then I'll have a look at what's
come in. Can I start with your name?

11
00:01:08,829 --> 00:01:10,600
Yes, it's Helen Prothero.

12
00:01:10,839 --> 00:01:12,940
And could you spell the surname for me?

13
00:01:13,374 --> 00:01:18,000
P-R-O-T-H-E-R-O.

14
00:01:19,069 --> 00:01:23,420
P-R-O-T-H-E-R-O-E?

15
00:01:23,669 --> 00:01:28,220
No, there's no E at the end. Everyone
adds one. It just finishes with the O.

16
00:01:28,404 --> 00:01:32,040
Sorry about that. I've taken it off.
And a contact number?

17
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:38,540
My mobile's best. It's 07712 648 309.

18
00:01:39,925 --> 00:01:45,320
07712 648 309. Lovely.
Now, when did you travel?

19
00:01:45,539 --> 00:01:49,300
It was Wednesday evening,
so that would be the 7th.

20
00:01:49,449 --> 00:01:51,720
The 7th. So that's two days ago?

21
00:01:52,014 --> 00:01:53,853
Oh, no, hang on.
I'm muddling it up.

22
00:01:53,877 --> 00:01:56,572
Wednesday was when I
travelled up for the conference.

23
00:01:57,099 --> 00:02:02,500
I came back yesterday on Thursday, so it
was the 8th. Sorry, it's been a long week.

24
00:02:02,594 --> 00:02:06,700
Not a problem. Thursday the 8th.
And do you know which train it was?

25
00:02:06,879 --> 00:02:10,175
It was from Redbourne to
Kingsford. I was booked on the 615,

26
00:02:10,199 --> 00:02:13,672
but the last session overran
and I missed it by about a minute.

27
00:02:14,275 --> 00:02:16,980
So I ended up getting
the next one, the 645.

28
00:02:18,010 --> 00:02:23,220
The 1845 from Redbourne. That's the
slow one, isn't it? Stops at every station.

29
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:24,940
It certainly felt like it.

30
00:02:25,115 --> 00:02:28,680
And was it a direct train, or did you
have to change anywhere along the way?

31
00:02:29,124 --> 00:02:31,860
Sometimes bags end up being
handed in at a different station.

32
00:02:32,105 --> 00:02:36,480
No, it was direct, thank goodness. I don't
think I could have coped with a change.

33
00:02:36,970 --> 00:02:39,622
I just sat there with
my eyes closed until

34
00:02:39,646 --> 00:02:42,692
Kingsford, and then,
well, I got off without it.

35
00:02:42,875 --> 00:02:44,969
Do you remember which
part of the train you were

36
00:02:44,993 --> 00:02:47,232
in? It helps the cleaning
teams know where to look.

37
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:52,980
Well, after the week I'd had, I'd have
loved to be in first class, but I wasn't.

38
00:02:53,485 --> 00:02:56,420
The standard carriages were
absolutely packed, so I went

39
00:02:56,444 --> 00:02:59,392
and found a seat in the
quiet coach, right at the front.

40
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:01,397
The quiet coach? That's useful.

41
00:03:01,421 --> 00:03:04,572
They're usually less cluttered,
so things get spotted.

42
00:03:04,970 --> 00:03:07,140
And where did you put the bag?
Under the seat?

43
00:03:07,420 --> 00:03:10,520
No, it's too bulky.
It wouldn't fit under the seat.

44
00:03:10,940 --> 00:03:13,360
I put it up on the luggage
rack, right above my head.

45
00:03:13,930 --> 00:03:17,000
That's exactly why I forgot it.
I couldn't see it.

46
00:03:17,190 --> 00:03:20,860
That's the most common one, honestly.
Out of sight, out of mind.

47
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:23,655
Before you hear the
rest of the conversation,

48
00:03:23,679 --> 00:03:26,312
you have some time to
look at questions 6 to 10.

49
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:02,800
Now listen and answer questions 6 to 10.

50
00:04:03,350 --> 00:04:06,693
Right. Now I need a
description of the bag itself.

51
00:04:06,717 --> 00:04:09,872
What sort of bag is it?
A suitcase? A rucksack?

52
00:04:12,740 --> 00:04:16,752
It's a hold-all. My son calls it my gym
bag, but it isn't really. I've never taken

53
00:04:16,776 --> 00:04:20,752
it near a gym. It's a proper hold-all with
two handles and a long shoulder strap.

54
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:23,520
A hold-all. And what colour is it?

55
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:29,419
Well, it looks black in some lights,
and the man at the station desk last night

56
00:04:29,443 --> 00:04:33,392
wrote down black, but it definitely
isn't. It's navy blue with a cream strap.

57
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:36,774
Navy blue, cream
strap. Is there anything

58
00:04:36,798 --> 00:04:40,032
that makes it stand
out? A name tag? A label?

59
00:04:46,420 --> 00:04:50,423
There used to be a name tag, but it tore off ages ago and I never
replaced it. But there's a little metal key ring on the zip. It's

60
00:04:50,447 --> 00:04:55,607
shaped like a lighthouse. My husband says it looks more like a
rocket, but it's a lighthouse. We bought it on holiday in Cornwall.

61
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:59,908
A lighthouse? That's actually really
helpful. It's those little details that let

62
00:04:59,932 --> 00:05:03,932
us match an item to the right person.
And what was inside it? Anything valuable?

63
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:14,410
Well, luckily my laptop wasn't in it. I'd put that in my
backpack. And my purse was in my coat pocket. But my camera was in

64
00:05:14,434 --> 00:05:19,514
there, and that's what I'm really upset about. All the photos
from the week are on it. The rest is just clothes and a paperback.

65
00:05:25,240 --> 00:05:29,170
I'll mark the camera as the main item of value. Let me just check the
system for yesterday evening. Nothing matching yet, I'm afraid. But

66
00:05:29,194 --> 00:05:33,546
please don't worry. Anything found on the late trains is collected at
the end of the line, and it usually reaches us here a day or two later.

67
00:05:33,570 --> 00:05:37,840
Oh, that's good to know. And if it
does turn up, how do I get it back?

68
00:05:38,180 --> 00:05:41,357
You can either come and collect
it from this office, at Kingsford

69
00:05:41,381 --> 00:05:45,012
Station, or we can post it to you.
There is a fee, I'm afraid, either way.

70
00:05:45,320 --> 00:05:46,880
How much are we talking about?

71
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:56,990
Gareth, to collect it in person, it's £12. It used to
be £10, but it went up in April. If you'd rather we

72
00:05:57,014 --> 00:06:01,216
posted it, it's the 12-plus postage, and for a bag that
size, you'd be looking at another £15 or so on top.

73
00:06:01,240 --> 00:06:05,421
Helen, in that case, I'll definitely
come and pick it up myself.

74
00:06:05,445 --> 00:06:08,032
I only live 20 minutes
away. £12 it is.

75
00:06:08,910 --> 00:06:12,846
Just bring some photo ID with you, a driving
licence or a passport, and we're open 8

76
00:06:12,870 --> 00:06:16,772
till 6, Monday to Saturday. And I'll ring
your mobile the moment anything comes in.

77
00:06:17,005 --> 00:06:20,660
Helen, thank you so much, Gareth.
You've been really kind.

78
00:06:21,760 --> 00:06:24,938
Part 2. You will hear a
council officer talking to

79
00:06:24,962 --> 00:06:28,472
local residents about a new
community allotment scheme.

80
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:32,760
First, you have some time
to look at questions 11 to 14.

81
00:07:05,140 --> 00:07:09,140
Now listen carefully and
answer questions 11 to 14.

82
00:07:09,710 --> 00:07:14,240
Ahem. Good evening, everyone, and thank
you for coming out on such a wet night.

83
00:07:14,705 --> 00:07:17,037
My name's Joanna Pike,
and I'm the Community

84
00:07:17,061 --> 00:07:19,512
Projects Officer at
Ashworth District Council.

85
00:07:19,900 --> 00:07:22,478
I'm here to tell you about
our new allotment scheme

86
00:07:22,502 --> 00:07:25,092
at Mill Lane, which opens
to plot holders in March.

87
00:07:25,570 --> 00:07:29,400
For those of you who don't know it, the
site used to be a commercial plant nursery.

88
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:32,640
It closed about six years ago,
and it's been empty ever since.

89
00:07:32,905 --> 00:07:35,460
Well, empty apart from a lot of brambles.

90
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:38,659
Now, quite a few of you have
asked why we went for Mill

91
00:07:38,683 --> 00:07:41,392
Lane rather than the field
behind the leisure centre,

92
00:07:41,700 --> 00:07:43,180
so let me deal with that first.

93
00:07:43,545 --> 00:07:47,260
I know some people were hoping the
new site would be easy to get to by bus,

94
00:07:47,515 --> 00:07:50,800
and I have to be honest, it
isn't ideal from that point of view.

95
00:07:51,140 --> 00:07:53,660
The nearest stop is a
good 15 minutes walk away.

96
00:07:54,055 --> 00:07:56,034
There's also a rumour
going round that the

97
00:07:56,058 --> 00:07:58,192
land was given to the
council free of charge.

98
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:00,200
That's not quite right, I'm afraid.

99
00:08:00,495 --> 00:08:04,652
We lease it from the Haddon Estate, although
admittedly, the rent is very modest.

100
00:08:05,100 --> 00:08:08,540
What actually decided it was,
first of all, the ground itself.

101
00:08:08,875 --> 00:08:11,129
When we had samples
analysed, the soil came

102
00:08:11,153 --> 00:08:13,472
back as being of
exceptionally high quality,

103
00:08:13,685 --> 00:08:17,200
which I suppose is the result of decades
of the nursery digging in compost.

104
00:08:17,675 --> 00:08:20,300
And the second thing, which
saved us a great deal of money,

105
00:08:20,555 --> 00:08:24,640
is that because it was a nursery, there's
already a mains water connection on site,

106
00:08:24,950 --> 00:08:27,580
so we haven't had to pay to
bring a supply in from the road.

107
00:08:27,930 --> 00:08:30,640
One thing Mill Lane is
not, though, is sheltered.

108
00:08:30,880 --> 00:08:34,299
The northern edge is very exposed,
and the winds there can be fierce,

109
00:08:34,649 --> 00:08:37,659
so we'll be planting a native hedge
along that side over the winter.

110
00:08:38,025 --> 00:08:39,025
Right, the money.

111
00:08:39,325 --> 00:08:43,059
A full plot will cost £60 a
year, and a half plot, £35.

112
00:08:43,709 --> 00:08:44,870
So, what do you get for that?

113
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:47,700
Well, people often assume
that water is included.

114
00:08:48,075 --> 00:08:49,075
It isn't, I'm afraid.

115
00:08:49,265 --> 00:08:51,140
Each row of plots has its own metre,

116
00:08:51,420 --> 00:08:54,420
and the cost is divided between
the plot holders in that row,

117
00:08:54,689 --> 00:08:56,000
so it's charged separately.

118
00:08:56,389 --> 00:09:00,740
What the fee does cover is unlimited use
of everything in the communal tool store.

119
00:09:01,130 --> 00:09:03,980
Spades, forks, wheelbarrows,
even a rotavator.

120
00:09:04,325 --> 00:09:08,680
And every autumn, the council will drop off
a load of well-rotted manure at every plot,

121
00:09:08,865 --> 00:09:12,520
which, if you've ever tried to buy the
stuff, you'll know is well worth having.

122
00:09:12,940 --> 00:09:15,313
In our pilot scheme
at Brook Street, we also

123
00:09:15,337 --> 00:09:17,832
gave out free packets
of seeds to new tenants,

124
00:09:18,155 --> 00:09:20,980
but sadly the budget won't
stretch to that this time round.

125
00:09:21,420 --> 00:09:24,880
And there's a gardeners' association,
which I'd really urge you to join,

126
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:27,260
but that's run by the
volunteers themselves,

127
00:09:27,475 --> 00:09:29,340
and it has its own small subscription,

128
00:09:29,760 --> 00:09:31,740
so it isn't part of what you pay us.

129
00:09:32,499 --> 00:09:34,140
Before you hear the rest of the talk,

130
00:09:34,305 --> 00:09:37,400
you have some time to
look at questions 15 to 20.

131
00:10:10,069 --> 00:10:16,920
Now, you should all have
a copy of the site plan,

132
00:10:17,280 --> 00:10:19,360
so let me take you
round the different areas,

133
00:10:19,595 --> 00:10:21,560
because each one has its own arrangements.

134
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:24,620
Starting at the main
gate on Mill Lane itself.

135
00:10:25,060 --> 00:10:26,660
You don't need to book a time to visit,

136
00:10:26,935 --> 00:10:29,560
you can come whenever you
like between dawn and dusk.

137
00:10:29,909 --> 00:10:33,920
At Brook Street, people got into the habit
of leaving the gate open during the day

138
00:10:33,921 --> 00:10:36,460
while they were working,
and we had several thefts.

139
00:10:36,760 --> 00:10:39,960
So, at Mill Lane, it has to
be kept locked at all times,

140
00:10:40,190 --> 00:10:41,340
even when you're on site.

141
00:10:41,680 --> 00:10:42,940
You'll each be given the code.

142
00:10:43,435 --> 00:10:45,280
Just inside the gate is the orchard.

143
00:10:45,545 --> 00:10:48,460
Twelve apple and plum trees
that the nursery left behind.

144
00:10:48,900 --> 00:10:52,040
We did consider allocating the
trees to individual plot holders,

145
00:10:52,380 --> 00:10:53,680
but we decided against it.

146
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,560
When the fruit's ripe, anyone who
has a plot is welcome to help themselves.

147
00:10:57,915 --> 00:10:59,340
Within reason, please.

148
00:10:59,709 --> 00:11:02,180
The volunteers prune the
trees on Saturday mornings,

149
00:11:02,475 --> 00:11:04,740
but that doesn't mean you
can only go in at weekends.

150
00:11:04,985 --> 00:11:06,140
It's open every day.

151
00:11:06,525 --> 00:11:08,820
In the middle of the site,
there's a wildlife pond.

152
00:11:09,165 --> 00:11:11,475
You might expect us to
have banned strimmers and

153
00:11:11,550 --> 00:11:13,880
mowers around it to
protect the frogs and newts,

154
00:11:14,135 --> 00:11:17,860
but in fact, our ecologist says
occasional cutting is perfectly fine.

155
00:11:18,185 --> 00:11:22,360
What we do insist on is that young children
never go near the water on their own.

156
00:11:22,645 --> 00:11:23,645
If you bring your kids,

157
00:11:23,805 --> 00:11:26,500
an adult must be with
them there at all times.

158
00:11:26,899 --> 00:11:31,120
Next to the car park, you'll see
eight raised beds, built at waist height.

159
00:11:31,121 --> 00:11:32,880
These aren't for general use.

160
00:11:32,881 --> 00:11:35,440
They're set aside for
gardeners who use a wheelchair,

161
00:11:35,441 --> 00:11:37,560
or who find kneeling
and bending difficult.

162
00:11:37,859 --> 00:11:41,120
There's a set of long-handled
tools stored right beside them too,

163
00:11:41,269 --> 00:11:44,000
so nobody has to carry heavy
equipment across the site.

164
00:11:44,349 --> 00:11:47,940
Beyond those is the polytunnel,
which is for raising seedlings in spring.

165
00:11:48,289 --> 00:11:51,380
It's unlocked all week, but
space on the benches is limited,

166
00:11:51,679 --> 00:11:53,500
so you can't just turn up with your trays.

167
00:11:53,860 --> 00:11:56,840
You'll need to reserve a bench in
advance through the online system.

168
00:11:57,245 --> 00:12:00,940
And finally, at the far end, by the
back gate, are the compost bays.

169
00:12:01,290 --> 00:12:03,820
A volunteer has to be
there to check what goes in,

170
00:12:04,025 --> 00:12:06,820
otherwise we end up with plastic
and goodness knows what else,

171
00:12:07,109 --> 00:12:09,340
so they're only open on
Saturdays and Sundays.

172
00:12:09,895 --> 00:12:11,340
And while we're down at that end,

173
00:12:11,700 --> 00:12:14,160
some of you will remember
the bonfires at Brook Street.

174
00:12:14,609 --> 00:12:17,820
Please be aware that they're not
permitted anywhere at Mill Lane,

175
00:12:18,039 --> 00:12:19,820
and that includes the compost area.

176
00:12:20,119 --> 00:12:21,840
The houses are just too close.

177
00:12:22,195 --> 00:12:24,400
Application forms are
on the table by the door.

178
00:12:24,625 --> 00:12:27,140
There's an open day on
the first Saturday in February,

179
00:12:27,305 --> 00:12:29,360
if you'd like to look
round before you sign up.

180
00:12:29,705 --> 00:12:31,760
Right, I think I've talked for long enough.

181
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:33,160
Are there any questions?

