00:13:33,769 --> 00:13:36,620
We were originally going to put
posters up all over campus, remember?

219
00:13:36,621 --> 00:13:40,620
And we got precisely
three volunteers out of that.

220
00:13:40,670 --> 00:13:41,670
Right.

221
00:13:41,994 --> 00:13:45,620
And the emails to the first year
class lists didn't do much better.

222
00:13:45,894 --> 00:13:49,620
It was really the item in the department's
newsletter that got us most of our people.

223
00:13:50,119 --> 00:13:52,620
Something like 30 of
the 40 came through that.

224
00:13:52,670 --> 00:13:53,620
Yeah.

225
00:13:53,621 --> 00:13:58,620
And I think we should be upfront about
it because it might affect who took part.

226
00:13:58,794 --> 00:14:02,620
People who actually read the newsletter
are probably more motivated than average.

227
00:14:02,621 --> 00:14:03,621
Good point.

228
00:14:03,769 --> 00:14:04,745
Okay.

229
00:14:04,769 --> 00:14:06,620
Then the learning session.

230
00:14:06,621 --> 00:14:09,620
Everyone studied the same
60 pairs of unrelated words.

231
00:14:09,621 --> 00:14:11,620
Where do we say that happened?

232
00:14:11,621 --> 00:14:13,620
Because it changed, didn't it?

233
00:14:13,769 --> 00:14:14,769
It did.

234
00:14:14,969 --> 00:14:18,620
We booked the lab, but then the
postgrads needed it for their own testing.

235
00:14:19,169 --> 00:14:21,620
And you suggested letting people
learn the words in their own rooms.

236
00:14:21,621 --> 00:14:23,620
Which would have been a disaster.

237
00:14:23,621 --> 00:14:26,620
No control over noise or anything.

238
00:14:27,019 --> 00:14:29,620
So, in the end, we used
that quiet room in the library.

239
00:14:29,769 --> 00:14:30,620
The one on the second floor?

240
00:14:30,621 --> 00:14:31,620
Yes.

241
00:14:31,621 --> 00:14:32,621
Okay.

242
00:14:32,869 --> 00:14:36,620
And then the sleep group went home
to bed and we measured their sleep.

243
00:14:37,219 --> 00:14:40,620
I still wish we'd been able to borrow
the EEG headset from the sleep lab.

244
00:14:40,621 --> 00:14:44,620
Josh, we weren't trained to use
it, and it would have cost a fortune.

245
00:14:44,621 --> 00:14:45,620
The wristband was fine.

246
00:14:45,621 --> 00:14:49,620
Everyone in the sleep group wore one
overnight and it gave us total sleep time.

247
00:14:49,621 --> 00:14:51,620
And the sleep diaries?

248
00:14:51,670 --> 00:14:54,063
We asked them to fill one
in, sure, but half of them

249
00:14:54,087 --> 00:14:56,627
didn't bother, so they really
weren't our main measure.

250
00:14:56,628 --> 00:14:58,620
I'd mention them in a footnote at most.

251
00:14:58,621 --> 00:14:59,620
Fair enough.

252
00:14:59,621 --> 00:15:03,620
Then, 12 hours after
learning, everyone came back.

253
00:15:03,719 --> 00:15:07,620
And before the actual memory
test, we did the alertness check.

254
00:15:07,719 --> 00:15:09,620
Which ended up being
just the questionnaire.

255
00:15:09,621 --> 00:15:12,723
We'd planned a reaction
time task as well, but it took

256
00:15:12,747 --> 00:15:15,627
15 minutes per person
and we didn't have the time.

257
00:15:15,628 --> 00:15:19,620
So, it was the questionnaire on
its own, rating how awake they felt.

258
00:15:19,621 --> 00:15:21,620
And then the recall test.

259
00:15:21,869 --> 00:15:24,795
Before you hear the
rest of the discussion, you

260
00:15:24,819 --> 00:15:27,632
have some time to look
at questions 25 to 30.

261
00:15:59,669 --> 00:16:03,620
Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30.

262
00:16:03,869 --> 00:16:05,620
Okay, results.

263
00:16:05,621 --> 00:16:08,620
So, the sleep group did
better, as we predicted.

264
00:16:08,919 --> 00:16:12,620
But honestly, what I didn't see
coming was how big the gap was.

265
00:16:12,621 --> 00:16:13,621
Really?

266
00:16:13,919 --> 00:16:18,620
The studies we read found roughly a
20% advantage for sleep, and ours was 18.

267
00:16:18,621 --> 00:16:20,620
That's pretty standard.

268
00:16:20,621 --> 00:16:21,620
No.

269
00:16:21,621 --> 00:16:24,620
What got me was where
the advantage came from.

270
00:16:24,819 --> 00:16:28,620
Nearly all of it was on the pairs that
people had rated as the hardest to learn.

271
00:16:28,969 --> 00:16:31,620
On the easy ones, the two
groups were almost level.

272
00:16:31,621 --> 00:16:32,620
Hmm.

273
00:16:32,621 --> 00:16:33,620
Yeah.

274
00:16:33,621 --> 00:16:37,620
When you put it like that, the overall
difference is in line with the literature.

275
00:16:37,794 --> 00:16:39,620
It's the hard pair thing that's odd.

276
00:16:40,169 --> 00:16:44,620
And the wake group did about as badly as we
expected, so there's nothing strange there.

277
00:16:44,719 --> 00:16:47,080
Speaking of the wake
group, I was worried that

278
00:16:47,104 --> 00:16:50,620
some of them might have had a nap during
the day, which would mess everything up.

279
00:16:50,621 --> 00:16:52,620
I don't think napping was the problem.

280
00:16:52,621 --> 00:16:55,620
We asked every one of them,
and nobody said they'd slept.

281
00:16:55,621 --> 00:16:58,050
And they were all
tested within 10 minutes

282
00:16:58,074 --> 00:17:00,632
of the right time, so
the timing's fine too.

283
00:17:00,633 --> 00:17:03,608
What does bother me is
the two who said they'd

284
00:17:03,632 --> 00:17:06,619
gone over the words in
their heads on the bus.

285
00:17:06,620 --> 00:17:07,620
Oh, yeah.

286
00:17:07,869 --> 00:17:10,619
They were actually
practicing them on purpose.

287
00:17:10,620 --> 00:17:12,619
That's a real issue.

288
00:17:13,019 --> 00:17:14,619
We should have told
everyone not to do that.

289
00:17:14,620 --> 00:17:15,619
Definitely.

290
00:17:15,620 --> 00:17:16,619
We'll mention it.

291
00:17:16,620 --> 00:17:18,619
In the limitations section?

292
00:17:18,869 --> 00:17:20,619
Or as some kind of confession?

293
00:17:21,619 --> 00:17:22,619
Limitations.

294
00:17:22,620 --> 00:17:26,619
Hollis appreciates honesty,
as long as it's in the right place.

295
00:17:26,620 --> 00:17:27,619
Now, limitations.

296
00:17:27,620 --> 00:17:31,620
I think the obvious criticism
Hollis will make is the sample size.

297
00:17:32,019 --> 00:17:33,620
40 people isn't many.

298
00:17:33,719 --> 00:17:37,620
Everyone says that, but 40's
not unusual for this kind of study.

299
00:17:38,319 --> 00:17:42,620
What worries me far more is that the sleep
group learned the words in the evening,

300
00:17:42,719 --> 00:17:44,620
and the wake group
learned them in the morning.

301
00:17:45,019 --> 00:17:47,620
So time of day is mixed up with sleep.

302
00:17:47,621 --> 00:17:48,620
We can't separate the two.

303
00:17:48,621 --> 00:17:52,620
And they were all
undergraduates, aged 18 to 22.

304
00:17:52,621 --> 00:17:54,620
That's a limitation, sure.

305
00:17:54,621 --> 00:17:56,620
But it's not specific to us.

306
00:17:56,621 --> 00:17:59,620
Most memory research uses students.

307
00:17:59,969 --> 00:18:01,620
It's the timing that's our own fault.

308
00:18:02,019 --> 00:18:05,620
Okay, but I don't think the timing
thing makes the whole study worthless.

309
00:18:05,621 --> 00:18:06,620
Far from it.

310
00:18:06,621 --> 00:18:09,620
And we couldn't have tested
everybody in the evening.

311
00:18:09,621 --> 00:18:13,620
The whole design needs a night's sleep for
one group and a day awake for the other.

312
00:18:13,621 --> 00:18:16,252
But look, the questionnaire
showed both groups

313
00:18:16,276 --> 00:18:18,632
felt equally alert
when they were tested.

314
00:18:19,019 --> 00:18:22,620
We can use that to argue the time of
day probably didn't make much difference.

315
00:18:22,621 --> 00:18:23,620
That's true.

316
00:18:23,621 --> 00:18:27,620
It doesn't solve it
completely, but it helps.

317
00:18:27,621 --> 00:18:28,621
Okay.

318
00:18:29,369 --> 00:18:30,620
What about participants 17?

319
00:18:30,621 --> 00:18:32,620
59 out of 60.

320
00:18:32,621 --> 00:18:35,620
My instinct is just to take them out.

321
00:18:35,621 --> 00:18:36,621
Josh!

322
00:18:36,869 --> 00:18:38,620
We can't just delete data
because it's inconvenient.

323
00:18:38,621 --> 00:18:41,620
Hollis would go through the roof.

324
00:18:41,769 --> 00:18:45,620
Well, could we email them and ask
whether they'd seen the list before?

325
00:18:45,621 --> 00:18:47,620
We promised everyone anonymity.

326
00:18:47,621 --> 00:18:50,620
We don't even have their
names linked to the numbers.

327
00:18:51,019 --> 00:18:52,620
Fair point.

328
00:18:53,019 --> 00:18:56,620
So we leave them in and flag it
up when we discuss the results.

329
00:18:57,319 --> 00:18:58,620
Page 68.

330
00:18:58,621 --> 00:18:59,620
Exactly.

331
00:18:59,621 --> 00:19:00,620
And then the presentation.

332
00:19:00,621 --> 00:19:03,214
I thought we'd spend
most of it on the wristbands

333
00:19:03,238 --> 00:19:05,632
since no other group
measured sleep that way.

334
00:19:05,819 --> 00:19:06,770
Hmm.

335
00:19:06,794 --> 00:19:08,620
It's only 10 minutes though.

336
00:19:08,621 --> 00:19:10,620
And the method's the least exciting part.

337
00:19:11,269 --> 00:19:13,620
I'd rather build it around
the hard pair result.

338
00:19:13,919 --> 00:19:15,620
That's what people will actually remember.

339
00:19:15,621 --> 00:19:16,620
Yeah.

340
00:19:16,621 --> 00:19:17,620
Okay.

341
00:19:17,621 --> 00:19:18,621
You've convinced me.

342
00:19:18,919 --> 00:19:20,620
And ideas for future studies?

343
00:19:20,621 --> 00:19:21,620
Just one slide.

344
00:19:21,621 --> 00:19:22,621
Right at the end.

345
00:19:22,819 --> 00:19:23,620
Nothing more.

346
00:19:23,621 --> 00:19:24,620
Great.

347
00:19:24,621 --> 00:19:26,620
So I'll draft the procedure tonight.

348
00:19:26,621 --> 00:19:27,620
You take the results.

349
00:19:27,621 --> 00:19:29,620
And we swap on Thursday
and check each other's sections?

350
00:19:29,621 --> 00:19:30,621
Deal.

351
00:19:30,994 --> 00:19:33,620
And we can put all the
wristband details in an appendix.

352
00:19:33,621 --> 00:19:35,620
So that work still gets some credit.

353
00:19:35,819 --> 00:19:36,670
Part 4.

354
00:19:36,694 --> 00:19:42,620
You will hear a lecturer giving a talk
about bioluminescence in deep sea animals.

355
00:19:43,369 --> 00:19:47,620
First, you have some time
to look at questions 31 to 40.

356
00:20:20,319 --> 00:20:24,620
Now listen carefully and
answer questions 31 to 40.

357
00:20:24,621 --> 00:20:25,621
Lecturer.

358
00:20:25,694 --> 00:20:26,620
Morning everyone.

359
00:20:26,621 --> 00:20:29,608
Today we're heading
down into the deep ocean,

360
00:20:29,632 --> 00:20:32,632
below about 200 meters,
where sunlight barely

361
00:20:32,869 --> 00:20:38,620
reaches, to look at bioluminescence, the
production of light by living organisms.

362
00:20:38,969 --> 00:20:41,620
It's not a rare curiosity, by the way.

363
00:20:41,621 --> 00:20:43,945
Down there, it's thought
that roughly three

364
00:20:43,969 --> 00:20:46,632
quarters of all animals
can make their own light.

365
00:20:47,219 --> 00:20:48,620
So, how is it done?

366
00:20:49,319 --> 00:20:52,620
In most cases, the light
comes from a chemical reaction.

367
00:20:52,719 --> 00:20:57,620
A light-emitting compound, known generally
as a luciferin, reacts with oxygen.

368
00:20:57,620 --> 00:21:01,620
And that reaction is speeded
up by an enzyme called luciferase.

369
00:21:02,169 --> 00:21:04,620
In some jellyfish, the
reaction is set off by calcium.

370
00:21:04,620 --> 00:21:09,620
And students often write that calcium
is what the luciferin combines with.

371
00:21:09,621 --> 00:21:10,620
It isn't.

372
00:21:10,620 --> 00:21:12,620
Calcium is only the trigger.

373
00:21:13,069 --> 00:21:15,620
What's remarkable is how efficient this is.

374
00:21:16,119 --> 00:21:19,620
An old-fashioned light bulb
loses most of its energy as heat.

375
00:21:19,620 --> 00:21:21,620
Bioluminescence loses almost none that way,

376
00:21:21,621 --> 00:21:24,620
which is why it's often
described as cold light.

377
00:21:25,519 --> 00:21:30,620
For an animal that may go weeks between
meals, that efficiency really matters.

378
00:21:30,621 --> 00:21:31,621
Then there's colour.

379
00:21:32,319 --> 00:21:34,957
In shallow coastal waters,
you'll find quite a lot of

380
00:21:34,981 --> 00:21:37,632
green light, and a handful
of species produce yellow.

381
00:21:38,269 --> 00:21:41,620
But in the deep ocean, the
light is overwhelmingly blue.

382
00:21:41,969 --> 00:21:44,620
And that's because blue wavelengths
travel the furthest through seawater.

383
00:21:44,621 --> 00:21:48,620
Most deep-sea animals, in
fact, can't even see other colours.

384
00:21:48,719 --> 00:21:51,620
And how do animals
switch the light on and off?

385
00:21:51,621 --> 00:21:53,620
Well, that varies.

386
00:21:54,269 --> 00:21:57,009
Many species control it
directly through their nervous

387
00:21:57,033 --> 00:21:59,632
system, so a flash can
last a fraction of a second.

388
00:22:00,269 --> 00:22:03,932
Others, which have glowing tissue
that's permanently lit, cover it with

389
00:22:03,956 --> 00:22:07,632
folds of skin, rather like shutters,
or rotate the light organ inwards.

390
00:22:07,633 --> 00:22:11,172
Either way, the light is under
the animal's control, and

391
00:22:11,196 --> 00:22:14,632
that's important for
everything I'm going to describe.

392
00:22:15,119 --> 00:22:18,620
I should add that not every glowing
animal makes its own chemicals.

393
00:22:18,819 --> 00:22:22,620
Some appear to get their luciferin
from their diet, from the food they eat.

394
00:22:22,669 --> 00:22:24,950
And others, the
anglerfish being the classic

395
00:22:24,974 --> 00:22:27,632
example, don't produce
the light themselves at all.

396
00:22:27,819 --> 00:22:32,632
Instead, they house bacteria inside
specialised organs, and it's those that glow.

397
00:22:33,369 --> 00:22:36,620
The fish supplies nutrients,
the bacteria supply light.

398
00:22:37,069 --> 00:22:38,620
A neat arrangement.

399
00:22:38,919 --> 00:22:40,620
Right, so that's the how.

400
00:22:40,819 --> 00:22:43,620
Now for the more interesting question: why?

401
00:22:44,069 --> 00:22:47,739
Why would an animal spend
energy, however little, on making

402
00:22:47,763 --> 00:22:51,632
light, when doing so might also
reveal it to something hungry?

403
00:22:51,869 --> 00:22:57,620
Biologists generally group the functions
into three: hiding, defending, and hunting.

404
00:22:57,969 --> 00:22:59,620
Plus one more I'll come to at the end.

405
00:23:00,269 --> 00:23:01,620
Let's start with hiding.

406
00:23:02,194 --> 00:23:06,620
In the upper part of the deep sea, there's
still a faint glow coming from the surface.

407
00:23:07,169 --> 00:23:09,857
Now, to a predator looking
up from below, it isn't an

408
00:23:09,881 --> 00:23:12,632
animal's colour, or even
its size, that gives it away.

409
00:23:12,633 --> 00:23:17,620
It's its silhouette, that dark
outline against the dim light above.

410
00:23:18,169 --> 00:23:21,382
So many fish and squid have
light organs along their bellies,

411
00:23:21,406 --> 00:23:24,632
and they adjust the brightness
to match the light from above.

412
00:23:24,919 --> 00:23:26,620
The outline simply disappears.

413
00:23:27,219 --> 00:23:29,620
This is called counter-illumination.

414
00:23:29,621 --> 00:23:30,621
Defence next.

415
00:23:30,669 --> 00:23:34,620
In shallow water, a squid that's
under attack squirts out ink.

416
00:23:34,719 --> 00:23:39,620
But think about it: in complete darkness,
a cloud of black ink is pretty useless.

417
00:23:39,620 --> 00:23:45,620
So some deep sea squid, and certain shrimps
too, release a glowing mucus instead.

418
00:23:45,669 --> 00:23:48,664
The predator is left
lunging at a bright, sticky

419
00:23:48,688 --> 00:23:51,632
cloud, while its prey
slips away into the dark.

420
00:23:51,769 --> 00:23:57,620
And then there's a rather clever strategy
that's been nicknamed the burglar alarm.

421
00:23:57,769 --> 00:24:01,627
Some jellyfish, when they're grabbed,
produce a spinning display of flashing light.

422
00:24:01,628 --> 00:24:04,620
You'd assume the aim
is to frighten the attacker.

423
00:24:05,219 --> 00:24:08,620
Well, it's not so much about
scaring it off, as attracting attention.

424
00:24:08,669 --> 00:24:12,544
The flashes can be seen a long
way off, and they bring in a larger

425
00:24:12,568 --> 00:24:16,620
predator, which may well go after
whatever's attacking the jellyfish.

426
00:24:16,621 --> 00:24:17,621
Now, hunting.

427
00:24:17,669 --> 00:24:21,222
The anglerfish I mentioned
dangles its glowing lure in front

428
00:24:21,246 --> 00:24:24,632
of its mouth, and curious
prey swims straight towards it.

429
00:24:24,633 --> 00:24:27,620
But my favourite example is the dragonfish.

430
00:24:27,744 --> 00:24:31,620
Remember I said most deep
sea animals only see blue?

431
00:24:32,119 --> 00:24:35,620
Well, the dragonfish has organs
under its eyes that give out red light.

432
00:24:35,621 --> 00:24:41,620
Its prey can't detect it, so the dragonfish
is effectively using a private searchlight.

433
00:24:42,469 --> 00:24:44,620
And the fourth function: communication.

434
00:24:44,620 --> 00:24:48,269
Lanternfish have rows of light
organs called photophores,

435
00:24:48,293 --> 00:24:51,632
and the pattern differs
from one species to another.

436
00:24:52,169 --> 00:24:54,335
For a long time, people
assumed these patterns

437
00:24:54,359 --> 00:24:56,632
mainly help the fish to
stay together in shoals.

438
00:24:57,469 --> 00:25:00,981
More recent work, though, suggests
that their real purpose is to help

439
00:25:01,005 --> 00:25:04,632
individuals recognize potential
mates of their own species in the dark.

440
00:25:05,519 --> 00:25:08,620
I should say that studying
all this is extremely difficult.

441
00:25:09,620 --> 00:25:13,399
Many of these animals are fragile, and
when they're hauled up in nets from several

442
00:25:13,423 --> 00:25:17,632
kilometers down, they rarely survive the journey,
and their light organs are often damaged.

443
00:25:17,769 --> 00:25:20,574
What's more, the moment
you shine a torch at them to

444
00:25:20,598 --> 00:25:23,632
take a photograph, you swamp
their own light completely.

445
00:25:26,819 --> 00:25:30,707
So, a lot of what we now know comes
from cameras on remotely operated vehicles,

446
00:25:30,731 --> 00:25:34,632
which can sit quietly in the dark for
hours and record extremely faint flashes.

447
00:25:34,969 --> 00:25:39,620
Finally, a word on why this research
matters outside marine biology.

448
00:25:39,719 --> 00:25:43,620
The genes for luciferase have
been put to use well beyond the sea.

449
00:25:43,669 --> 00:25:46,220
One early application
was in tests to detect

450
00:25:46,244 --> 00:25:48,632
bacteria in food,
and that still goes on.

451
00:25:49,069 --> 00:25:51,897
But by far their most
important use today is

452
00:25:51,921 --> 00:25:54,632
in medical research,
especially on cancer.

453
00:25:55,069 --> 00:25:58,304
Scientists insert the gene
into cancer cells so that they

454
00:25:58,328 --> 00:26:01,632
can watch, in a living animal,
how tumors grow and spread.

455
00:26:01,633 --> 00:26:06,620
So, from the bottom of the ocean to
the hospital, which is quite a journey.

